= 1. The Historical Transition of the 1990s: Five Crucial Developments

The year 1989 marks the decisive end of the single-party "Congress System" at the Centre and the beginning of a quarter-century era of multi-party coalition governance in India. Political scientists view the decade of the 1990s as a foundational turning point shaped by five interconnected developments:

1. *The Collapse of Congress Dominance (1989):* In the Ninth General Elections of November 1989, the Congress party's parliamentary tally plunged from 415 seats (won in 1984) to 197 seats. Although Congress remained the single largest party, it lacked an absolute majority and declined to stake a claim, inaugurating the era of coalition politics where no single party secured an absolute majority in the Lok Sabha until 2014.
2. *The Mandal Issue & Rise of OBC Politics:* The implementation of the Second Backward Classes Commission recommendations in August 1990 by Prime Minister V.P. Singh sparked nationwide student protests and permanently altered the social composition of political representation, ushering in the political empowerment of the Other Backward Classes (OBCs).
3. *Structural Economic Reforms (1991):* Confronting an acute balance-of-payments crisis where foreign exchange reserves dropped to barely two weeks of imports, Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh initiated historic structural adjustment reforms—ushering in Liberalisation, Privatisation, and Globalisation (LPG)—dismantling the Nehruvian "License-Permit-Quota Raj".
4. *The Ayodhya Dispute and Rise of Hindutva:* The unlocking of the gates of the disputed Babri structure in 1986, L.K. Advani's Somnath-to-Ayodhya *Rath Yatra* in 1990, and the demolition of the 16th-century Babri Masjid on 6 December 1992 precipitated communal violence and elevated cultural nationalism (Hindutva) into a dominant axis of national electoral competition.
5. *The Assassination of Rajiv Gandhi (May 1991):* During an election rally in Sriperumbudur, Tamil Nadu, former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi was assassinated by a suicide bomber of the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE). This shifted national sympathy towards Congress, enabling P.V. Narasimha Rao to lead a minority government that governed for a full five years (1991–1996).

