= 2. The Rise of Janata Dal and the National Front (1989–1990)

The political alternative to Congress was forged through the consolidation of centrist and agrarian opposition forces:

== Formation of the Janata Dal

- *The Bofors Catalyst:* In 1987, Vishwanath Pratap Singh (V.P. Singh), Union Finance and Defense Minister in Rajiv Gandhi's cabinet, resigned following public allegations of kickbacks and corruption in the procurement of Swedish Bofors 155mm howitzer guns.
- *The Merger:* V.P. Singh founded the *Jan Morcha*. In October 1988, the Jan Morcha merged with the Janata Party, Lok Dal (Chaudhary Devi Lal and Ajit Singh factions), and Congress (S) to establish the *Janata Dal*.
- *The National Front:* The Janata Dal formed an alliance with prominent regional parties—including the DMK in Tamil Nadu, Telugu Desam Party (TDP) in Andhra Pradesh, and Asom Gana Parishad (AGP) in Assam—under the banner of the *National Front*.

== The National Front Government (1989–1990)

- *The Unique Ideological Geometry:* Following the 1989 elections, V.P. Singh was elected leader and sworn in as Prime Minister. The National Front commanded only 143 seats and was sustained in power by a unprecedented external floor alliance: supported from the outside simultaneously by the right-wing *Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP, 85 seats)* and the *Left Front (45 seats)*. Both supporting blocs declined to join the cabinet, sitting on opposite sides of the treasury benches.
- *Internal Factional Discord:* Deputy Prime Minister Chaudhary Devi Lal, representing the powerful agrarian peasant lobby of northern India, frequently clashed with V.P. Singh. When Devi Lal was sacked from the Union Cabinet on 1 August 1990, he announced a massive peasant rally in New Delhi. To outmaneuver Devi Lal's rural mobilization and consolidate an unassailable backward-class vote base, V.P. Singh made the sudden announcement in Parliament on *7 August 1990* that his government would implement the recommendations of the Mandal Commission.
- *The Fall of the Government (November 1990):* In response to the Mandal mobilization, BJP President L.K. Advani embarked on a nationwide *Rath Yatra* to mobilize Hindu public opinion behind the Ram Janmabhoomi movement. When Advani was arrested in Samastipur on the orders of Bihar Chief Minister Lalu Prasad Yadav in October 1990, the BJP formally withdrew its outside support. V.P. Singh lost a vote of confidence on 7 November 1990 by 142 to 346 votes, ending his 11-month tenure.

== Chandra Shekhar's Minority Government (1990–1991)

Following V.P. Singh's resignation, *Chandra Shekhar* broke away from the Janata Dal with 64 MPs to establish the *Samajwadi Janata Party (Rashtriya)*. Chandra Shekhar assumed office as Prime Minister with outside floor support provided by Rajiv Gandhi's Congress (197 MPs). However, the fragile arrangement collapsed within four months when Congress accused the Haryana government of deploying plainclothes constables to spy outside Rajiv Gandhi's residence, prompting Chandra Shekhar to resign in March 1991.

= 3. The Mandal Commission and Other Backward Classes (OBC)

Affirmative action in post-independence India was originally confined to the Scheduled Castes (SC) and Scheduled Tribes (ST). The constitutional struggle to extend reservations to the intermediate agrarian and artisan castes—collectively designated the *Other Backward Classes (OBCs)*—transformed the social hierarchy of Indian democracy.

== Constitutional Origins and Prior Commissions

- *Article 340 Mandate:* Article 340 of the Constitution empowers the President to appoint a commission to investigate the conditions of socially and educationally backward classes and recommend remedial measures.
- *The First Backward Classes Commission (1953):* Chaired by veteran Gandhian *Kaka Kalelkar*, the First Commission submitted its report in 1955 identifying 2,399 backward castes. However, the report was shelved by the Central government due to internal dissensions (including Kalelkar's own ambiguous dissenting note) and the lack of quantifiable, objective criteria.
- *Southern Precedents & The Karpoori Thakur Formula:* While northern India lagged, southern states had instituted backward-class reservations decades earlier. In Bihar, socialist Chief Minister *Karpoori Thakur* introduced a pioneering layered reservation formula in 1978 allocating quotas separately for Other Backward Castes (12%), Extremely Backward Castes (8%), women (3%), and economically backward upper castes (3%), serving as a direct precursor to national OBC affirmative action.

== The Second Backward Classes Commission (Mandal Commission)

- *Appointment (1978):* In December 1978, the Morarji Desai-led Janata Party government appointed the Second Backward Classes Commission under the chairmanship of *Bindeshwari Prasad Mandal (B.P. Mandal)*, former Chief Minister of Bihar.
- *Scientific Methodology & 11 Indicators:* The Mandal Commission devised 11 indicators grouped into three broad dimensions:
  1. *Social Indicators (4):* Castes considered socially backward by others; dependent on manual labor; high female labor participation; early marriage rates.
  2. *Educational Indicators (3):* Low school attendance; higher dropout rates; lower proportion of matriculates.
  3. *Economic Indicators (4):* Family assets below state average; living in kachha dwellings; consumption debt; lack of access to drinking water.
- *Key Findings:* The Commission identified *3,743 castes* as backward, estimating that the OBCs constituted approximately *52% of India's total population* (excluding SCs and STs).
- *The 27% Quota Recommendation:* While the OBC population stood at 52%, the Commission recommended a *27% reservation* in Central government civil posts, public sector undertakings, and higher educational institutions, ensuring that total reservations (22.5% for SC/ST + 27% for OBC = 49.5%) stayed strictly within the 50% ceiling established by Supreme Court jurisprudence in the *M.R. Balaji (1963)* case.
- *Submission (1980):* The Mandal Commission submitted its landmark report in December 1980, but it remained unaddressed during the Indira Gandhi and Rajiv Gandhi administrations until V.P. Singh announced its implementation on 7 August 1990.

== Implementation and the Indra Sawhney Judgment (1992)

V.P. Singh's decision ignited furious upper-caste student protests, violent demonstrations, and tragic self-immolations across northern India. The constitutional validity of the 27% quota was challenged before the Supreme Court.

- *The 9-Judge Constitutional Bench:* In the historic *Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992)* ruling (popularly known as the Mandal Case), the Supreme Court upheld the validity of the 27% OBC reservation by a 6:3 majority.
- *Core Judicial Directives:*
  1. *Validity of Caste:* Caste can be a valid criterion for determining social backwardness under Article 16(4).
  2. *The 50% Rule:* Total reservations in regular recruitments must not exceed *50%*, except under extraordinary regional circumstances.
  3. *The "Creamy Layer" Exclusion:* Advanced, socially and economically affluent individuals among the OBCs (e.g., children of constitutional dignitaries, Class I civil servants, large landowners, or high-income earners) must be excluded from affirmative action benefits.
  4. *No Quotas in Promotions:* Article 16(4) permits reservation only at the stage of initial entry into service, not in promotional posts. (Parliament later amended the Constitution via Articles 16(4A) and 16(4B) to protect SC/ST promotions).
- *Subsequent Educational Extensions:* In 2006, the UPA government enacted the Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Admission) Act, extending the 27% OBC quota to central universities, IITs, and IIMs, which was upheld by the Supreme Court in *Ashoka Kumar Thakur v. Union of India (2008)*. In 2017, the Union appointed the *Justice G. Rohini Commission* under Article 340 to examine the sub-categorization of OBCs.

