= 4. Bahujan Resurgence and the Rise of Caste-Based Regional Parties

The implementation of Mandal and the political mobilization of marginalized castes revolutionized state-level party competition:

== Kanshi Ram and the Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP)

- *BAMCEF (1978):* In 1978, Dalit social reformer *Kanshi Ram* organized government employees from backward and minority communities into the *Backward and Minority Communities Employees Federation (BAMCEF)*, building an autonomous network of educated subaltern professionals.
- *DS-4 (1981):* In 1981, Kanshi Ram established the *Dalit Shoshit Samaj Sangharsh Samiti (DS-4)* to conduct direct mass agitational campaigns against caste discrimination.
- *Formation of BSP (1984):* In April 1984, Kanshi Ram launched the *Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP)*, aiming to unite the *Bahujan Samaj*—comprising Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Other Backward Classes, and religious minorities (who together formed 85% of the population)—to wrest political power from the upper-caste minority.
- *Mayawati's Rise:* Under Kanshi Ram's mentorship, *Mayawati* emerged as a towering Dalit leader, becoming the Chief Minister of Uttar Pradesh in June 1995 (heading a coalition with the Samajwadi Party and BJP support), the first Dalit woman to head a state government in India.

== Agrarian OBC Regional Formations

Mandal mobilization catalyzed powerful regional parties dominated by agrarian middle castes:
- *Samajwadi Party (SP):* Founded by *Mulayam Singh Yadav* in 1992 in Uttar Pradesh, building a formidable coalition of intermediate agrarian castes (Yadavas) and Muslim minorities.
- *Rashtriya Janata Dal (RJD):* Founded by *Lalu Prasad Yadav* in Bihar in 1997, commanding immense mass loyalty through the "MY" (Muslim-Yadav) social coalition and unyielding secularist politics.
- *Janata Dal (United) [JD(U)]:* Led by *Nitish Kumar* and George Fernandes, mobilizing Extremely Backward Classes (EBCs) and non-Yadav peasant communities in Bihar.

= 5. United Front Governments (1996–1998)

The 11th General Election of May 1996 produced a fractured mandate. The BJP emerged as the single largest party with 161 seats:

- *Vajpayee's 13-Day Government (May 1996):* President Shankar Dayal Sharma invited Atal Bihari Vajpayee to form the government. Unable to secure alliance partners due to the BJP's political untouchability over the Babri demolition, Vajpayee delivered an emotional televised farewell address in the Lok Sabha and resigned after *13 days* without facing a formal vote of confidence.
- *The United Front Coalition:* A coalition of 13 regional, socialist, and secular parties—including the Janata Dal, Samajwadi Party, DMK, TDP, AGP, and the Left Front—formed the *United Front (UF)*. When veteran Marxist Chief Minister Jyoti Basu was barred by the CPI(M) Politburo from accepting the Prime Ministership (which Basu later termed a *"historic blunder"*), Karnataka Chief Minister *H.D. Deve Gowda* was chosen as the compromise Prime Minister.
- *Outside Support by Congress:* The United Front commanded only 180 seats and survived solely on the external floor support of the Congress party (140 seats) and the CPI (which joined the cabinet).
- *The Gujral Administration (1997–1998):* In March 1997, Congress President Sitaram Kesri withdrew support from Deve Gowda. The United Front reconstituted itself under seasoned diplomat *Inder Kumar Gujral*. Gujral formulated the famous *Gujral Doctrine*, which posited that in its relations with smaller South Asian neighbors (Bangladesh, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Bhutan, Maldives), India—as the largest regional power—must extend unilateral concessions without seeking reciprocal favors. The Gujral government collapsed in November 1997 when Congress demanded the expulsion of DMK ministers following the Jain Commission's interim report on the assassination of Rajiv Gandhi.

= 6. The National Democratic Alliance (NDA: 1998–2004)

The 12th General Elections of 1998 saw the BJP shed its political isolation by stitching together a vast pre-poll and post-poll confederation of regional parties named the *National Democratic Alliance (NDA)*:

- *The 13-Month Government (1998–1999):* Atal Bihari Vajpayee took oath as Prime Minister in March 1998. The government conducted the historic *Pokhran-II* nuclear tests (*Operation Shakti*) on 11 and 13 May 1998, establishing India as a declared nuclear-weapon state. In April 1999, the AIADMK under J. Jayalalithaa withdrew its 18 MPs, and the Vajpayee government fell by a solitary vote (*269 to 270*) on the floor of the Lok Sabha.
- *Diplomacy and Conflict (1999–2002):* Vajpayee traveled to Pakistan on the inaugural Delhi-Lahore bus in February 1999, signing the historic Lahore Declaration. However, Pakistani forces infiltrated Kargil months later, prompting the Kargil War (Operation Vijay, May–July 1999) where Indian armed forces evicted all intruders. Following the 13 December 2001 Parliament terrorist attack, India launched Operation Parakram and enacted the Prevention of Terrorism Act (POTA) in 2002.
- *The Full Five-Year Term (1999–2004):* In the October 1999 elections, the NDA swept to power with 303 seats. Vajpayee headed a 24-party coalition that governed until May 2004, becoming the *first non-Congress government in Indian history to complete a full five-year term*.
- *Major Policy Achievements:* National Highway Development Project (NHDP: Golden Quadrilateral and North-South/East-West Corridors), telecom deregulation under the New Telecom Policy (1999), the launch of *Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan*, and targeted disinvestment of public sector enterprises.

= 7. The United Progressive Alliance (UPA: 2004–2014)

Defying exit polls and the NDA's "India Shining" campaign, the May 2004 general elections returned the Congress as the single largest party with 145 seats:

- *The Creation of UPA:* Congress forged a post-poll coalition named the *United Progressive Alliance (UPA)*. In an emotional meeting of the Congress Parliamentary Party, Congress President Sonia Gandhi renounced the office of Prime Minister, nominating eminent economist and former Finance Minister *Dr. Manmohan Singh*, who was sworn in as Prime Minister.
- *UPA-I (2004–2009): Rights-Based Legislative Revolution:* Supported from the outside by the Left Front (61 MPs), UPA-I adopted a National Common Minimum Programme (NCMP) and enacted transformative welfare legislation:
  1. *Right to Information Act (RTI, 2005):* Empowered citizens to inspect government records, revolutionising transparency.
  2. *MGNREGA (2005):* Guaranteed 100 days of statutory wage employment per year to every rural household.
  3. *Forest Rights Act (2006):* Restored traditional land and forest rights to Scheduled Tribes and traditional forest dwellers.
- *The 2008 Nuclear Trust Vote:* When the Left Front withdrew support over the Indo-US Civil Nuclear Agreement in July 2008, the government survived a tense parliamentary confidence vote with support from the Samajwadi Party.
- *UPA-II (2009–2014):* In 2009, UPA was re-elected with an expanded tally of 262 seats (Congress winning 206 seats alone). It enacted the *Right to Education Act (RTE, 2009)* and the *National Food Security Act (NFSA, 2013)*, but was severely weakened by administrative paralysis and high-profile anti-corruption agitations (India Against Corruption led by Anna Hazare).

= 8. NDA III & IV: The Dominant Coalition Era (2014–2024)

The 16th General Election of May 2014 shattered the 25-year-old precedent of fractured mandates:

- *The 2014 Mandate:* The BJP under Narendra Modi secured *282 seats* on its own (commanding an absolute majority in the 543-member Lok Sabha), while the NDA secured *336 seats*. This was the first time since 1984 that any single party won a standalone majority.
- *The 2019 Mandate:* In May 2019, the BJP expanded its standalone majority to *303 seats* (NDA winning 353 seats).
- *Conceptualizing the "Dominant Coalition":* Political scientists, most notably Suhas Palshikar, characterized this phase as the *"Second Dominant Party System"* or the *"BJP-System"*. Unlike the fragile coalitions of 1989–2014 where the lead party was held hostage by regional allies, NDA III and IV functioned as a "dominant coalition" where the principal party commanded an independent parliamentary majority while preserving the multi-party coalition format for wider federal legitimacy.

= 9. Yogendra Yadav's Three Democratic Upsurges

Eminent political scientist Yogendra Yadav formulated an influential conceptual model tracing the deepening of Indian democracy through three distinct "Democratic Upsurges":

